 ## Background
-The extension of NeRF to dynamic scenes originated with the need to capture human performance and articulated objects from limited camera views [G]. Early works like [A-NeRF](18108-a-nerf.md) addressed the ill-posed nature of monocular dynamic reconstruction by introducing skeletal-relative encodings and joint optimization of pose and appearance [S2]. Subsequent methods like [MoCo-NeRF](16197-moco-nerf.md) and [SelfNeRF](18160-selfnerf.md) refined this by decoupling rigid and non-rigid components or leveraging surface geometry for faster convergence. Generalizable approaches like Neural Human Performer [S1, unofficial] and HOSNeRF [S2] further expanded the domain to sparse multi-view and single-view human-object interactions, respectively.
+The extension of NeRF to dynamic scenes originated with the need to capture human performance and articulated objects from limited camera views [G]. Early works like [A-NeRF](18108-a-nerf.md) addressed the ill-posed nature of monocular dynamic reconstruction by introducing skeletal-relative encodings and joint optimization of pose and appearance [S2]. Subsequent methods like [MoCo-NeRF](16197-moco-nerf.md) and [SelfNeRF](18160-selfnerf.md) refined this by decoupling rigid and non-rigid components or leveraging surface geometry for faster convergence [G]. Generalizable approaches like Neural Human Performer [S1, unofficial] and HOSNeRF [S2] further expanded the domain to sparse multi-view and single-view human-object interactions, respectively.
 
